{%- comment -%}
  PG Cart Open: stop pg-no-cart-page hijacking the cart icon.

  THE BUG IT CAUSES. pg-no-cart-page intercepts every click on a /cart link in
  the capture phase and does this:

      e.preventDefault();                          // blocks the theme's drawer
      var opened = openDrawer(a);
      if (!opened) window.location.href = a.href;  // gives up, navigates

  openDrawer() looks for the drawer trigger with

      querySelectorAll('a[href*="cart"]')  ->  first one not inside #cart

  which is the anchor that was just clicked. It then refuses to use it:

      if (a && a !== skip)   // a === skip, so this is false

  so `opened` is always false on that click, and the handler force-navigates to
  /cart. Every time. The theme's own drawer never gets the event, because
  preventDefault already ate it.

  That is the flash: block the drawer, fail to open it by hand, navigate to
  /cart, and let pg-cart-gone bounce back. Then openIfFlagged() fires on arrival,
  dispatches another synthetic click on the same anchor, the same interceptor
  catches it, and the round trip happens again. Hence a cart that "spazzes"
  between the old page and the drawer instead of just opening.

  The file's own comment says the drawer "already opens from it on every page",
  which is the point: the native behaviour worked, and this broke it.

  THE FIX. window.pgNoCartPage is that file's own re-entry guard --

      if (window.pgNoCartPage) return; window.pgNoCartPage = 1;

  -- so setting it first makes the whole IIFE bail before it binds anything. It
  is stamped here rather than by editing that 6KB file, so the change is one
  line to revert. This renders from pg-cart-fast, 4th in footer-group, while
  pg-no-cart-page is 23rd; scripts run in document order, so the flag is always
  set before it is read.

  With the interceptor gone, clicking the cart icon does what it did before any
  of this existed: the theme opens the drawer, and no navigation happens at all.

  WHAT STILL COVERS /cart. pg-cart-gone, which renders as the entire cart
  template and redirects during parse. Bookmarks, old links and anything this
  does not catch still land back on the site rather than on a second cart. That
  is the safety net, and it is earlier and simpler than the one being disabled.

  The one thing deliberately not carried over is openIfFlagged(), which re-opened
  the drawer after a bounce by dispatching a click at an anchor. Dispatching a
  click at an <a href="/cart"> only avoids navigating if something cancels the
  event -- which is exactly the assumption that failed above -- so a direct visit
  to /cart now simply returns you to the site, and the cart icon opens the drawer
  when you want it. No synthetic clicks, nothing to loop.

  The visibility rule is neutralised too: that CSS hides #content on the cart
  template and relies on the script it no longer runs to reveal it. Without this,
  the <noscript> fallback in pg-cart-gone would be invisible.
{%- endcomment -%}
<script>window.pgNoCartPage = 1;</script>
<style>
  body.template-cart #content{visibility:visible !important}
</style>
